alex: Hey folks — running into a nasty ESM/CJS interop thing when deploying the JS SDK in a Next.js Edge function. Runtime throws `SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module`. Project is ESM (\"type\": \"module\") so surprised.

maria: Can you paste the exact import and package.json fields? Sometimes the bundler or node resolution picks a CJS entry point even in ESM projects.

alex: Import line is `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'`. package.json has `type: module`. Minimal edge route: ```js
// app/edge/route.js
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'
export default async (req) => {
  const r = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY })
  return new Response(await r.generate('hi'))
}
```
Error stack points to a `require()` call inside a transitive helper in the SDK.

sam: That matches an exports map mismatch. If package.json `exports` has only a cjs index or the `main` field points to CJS, ESM bundlers can still resolve the CJS file. Edge runtimes (Vercel/Cloudflare) expect published packages to include an `import`/`module` ESM entry.

jin: Also worth noting: process.env isn't reliably available in some edge sandboxes. Passing secrets via environment may not work the same way as Node serverless. You can test by injecting a header or using platform secrets.

alex: I tried `globalThis.process = { env: { REDWOOD_KEY: 'x' } }` as a quick hack and auth proceeds, but that is gross. If the SDK loads CJS `require()` at import-time it breaks bundlers.

maria: For TypeScript consumers, `const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference')` vs `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'` causes typing pain (default vs named export). We should document both patterns and preferred Edge approach.

sam: Short-term workaround: import the explicit ESM entry from the package if available, e.g. `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference/dist/esm/index.mjs'`. Not great to rely on internal paths but unblocks developers.

jin: I opened an issue: https://github.com/redwood-inference/js-sdk/issues/142 — suggested fixes: 1) ensure package.json `exports` has \"import\" mapping to ESM build and `types` reference on that entry, 2) ship a pure-ESM bundle for edge with .d.ts, 3) add an auth helper that accepts `apiKey` or header so edge examples are explicit.

alex: Another problem: when bundling for Cloudflare with esbuild I get `Could not find a declaration file for module '@redwood/inference'`. So types not referenced from ESM export.

maria: Ok actionable list: 1) Docs: add \"Edge runtimes\" section showing import variants, auth header example, and note about process.env differences; 2) Packaging: add `exports.import`, `module`, and point `types` at ESM d.ts; 3) SDK: make runtime detection less opinionated (accept fetchFn, explicit apiKey param) and avoid top-level `require()` calls. 4) Quick note about retries: worker runtimes sometimes don't have setTimeout or different fetch timeouts — document retry caveats.

alex: I'll draft a docs PR. Proposed Edge example to include: ```js
// recommended edge snippet
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'
export default async (req) => {
  const apiKey = req.headers.get('x-redwood-key') // inject with platform secrets
  const client = new Redwood({ apiKey, fetchFn: fetch }) // pass platform fetch
  const out = await client.generate('hello from edge')
  return new Response(out)
}
```

sam: Great. devx-bot: linking docs page and issue: <https://redwood.inference/docs/js-sdk-edge> and https://github.com/redwood-inference/js-sdk/issues/142 :thumbsup:

devx-bot: Added label `edge-docs` and assigned to @alex.

alex: Thanks — will post PR link when ready.
